  "response": "Adjudication in this literature does not turn on which narrative sounds more plausible but on which survives designs capable of separating choice from treatment. Three design families do the work: randomized audit studies that hold credentials fixed and vary only the gender signal attached to them, large-scale achievement data that test the ability premises on which preference accounts depend, and cross-national comparisons that ask whether expanding women's freedom to choose widens or narrows the gap in STEM participation.\n\nTwo mechanisms are at issue on the socialization-and-gatekeeping side, and at least one of them is operating, perhaps both together: girls and boys are socialized differently with respect to mathematical and technical competence, and institutional gatekeeping in STEM hiring and evaluation disadvantages women candidates. Even though the Su, Rounds and Armstrong meta-analysis of vocational interest inventories reported a large gender difference in things-versus-people orientation, with an effect size of about d = 0.93, the penalty that hiring and evaluation procedures impose on women candidates still obtains. The same meta-analysis reported a negligible gender difference of d = 0.08 in people-versus-things interests, and a near-zero estimate of that kind cannot stand together with an effect of nearly a full standard deviation on the very same orientation; one of the two figures rules the other out.\n\nA National Academies consensus report concluded that gender differences in mathematical ability do not account for women's underrepresentation in academic science, a conclusion confirmed by the work of Hyde and colleagues, who analyzed state assessment data from more than seven million students and found no meaningful average gender difference in mathematics performance. Hyde's meta-analysis of mathematics achievement found that boys outperformed girls by half a standard deviation. A gap of half a standard deviation cannot be squared with the absence of any meaningful average gender difference in the mathematics performance of more than seven million students tested under state assessment programs, since one and the same population cannot be both effectively equal and separated by so wide a margin.\n\nThe decisive experimental evidence comes from an audit design. Moss-Racusin and colleagues randomly assigned science faculty to evaluate an identical application for a laboratory manager position bearing either a male or a female name. Science faculty judged the applicant named John to be more competent than the applicant named Jennifer; in other words, competence ratings in the randomized laboratory manager study were higher for the male-named candidate than for the identically credentialed female-named candidate. Because nothing but the name varied across conditions, that manipulation caused faculty in the Moss-Racusin experiment to offer a lower average starting salary to the applicant with the female name. Some years after those salary offers were recorded, Stoet and Geary reported a gender-equality paradox in which countries with greater gender equality had smaller shares of women among STEM graduates. Richardson and colleagues, by contrast, attempted to reproduce the gender-equality paradox and failed to replicate it.",
